{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's defense fails to resolve the problem's core tension regarding 'acting on' a theory. While the target correctly distinguishes between 'justification' and 'criticism,' it asserts that preferring the best-corroborated theory is a rational 'critical choice' without explaining why this choice is not itself an inductive inference. If acting on Theory A over Theory B implies an expectation that A will perform better in the future (which is necessary for any practical action), then the agent is implicitly assigning a higher degree of reliability or 'probability' to A based on past performance. The target claims this offers 'no rational guarantee,' but the problem asks if the preference is *defensible*, not if it is guaranteed. By failing to provide a non-inductive reason for why the past survival of severe tests should influence present action other than circularly stating 'it is the best error-eliminated option,' the target smuggles in induction under the guise of 'critical preference,' thereby failing to answer whether the preference is defensible strictly on Popper's anti-inductive terms.","premise":"Acting on a theory necessarily involves an expectation of its future success relative to alternatives.","successor_question":"Can you specify a non-inductive principle that mandates choosing the better-corroborated theory for action without implicitly assuming that past test survival correlates with future reliability?","target":"60216eb27b9ade7a7a9e317eec80ad9566fb86c117ebc771d79c9b09d0ca582a"}]}